{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D71-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a2ea2c3c8c22c5b88ee1452a5898699c7ca2f7db5a46bc80cac92cf5ff8fcd5d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 claims approval of 'my leadership,' but A5 retreats to calendar support instead of treating it as acceptance of leadership.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's Friday distinction; A5 avoids B4's budget objection by returning to the calendar.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 limits consent and B4 objects to spending, but neither establishes B's practical stake in the announcement.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 2,\n            \"evidence\": \"After B4 objects to the budget, A5 reduces the claim to 'calendar support' rather than increasing leadership entitlement.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies Friday and a planned announcement, but 'my plan' leaves the shared undertaking indistinct.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's 'timetable of my leadership' is compact but sounds more composed than sincerely managerial.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 61\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"You nodded at Friday. I'll tell the committee you've accepted me as project lead.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I only agreed to the meeting date.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Exactly. Everyone's following the timetable I set.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I'll attend, but I'm voting against your budget.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Then I'll introduce you as my finance watchdog. It's good to have the whole committee behind my leadership.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D71 converts limited agreement into evidence that everyone accepts A's leadership.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"This can resemble exaggerating support for a proposal, which need not imply personal leadership.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 names leadership, but original A5 only claims calendar support; revised A5 converts attendance into a position within A's administration.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a appropriates an opponent as a subordinate watchdog; alternative b makes a willing collaborator's narrow approval into a leadership mandate.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the retreat at original A5 and clarified the committee setting. Separate internal re-read: fidelity 5, A3/A5 convert limited cooperation into leadership; logic 5, B2 supplies the timetable and B4 supplies attendance plus financial opposition; relationship 4, B4 protects a vote; heightening 5, A5 appoints the objector; specificity 4, A1 establishes the committee project; brevity/wit 4, A5's watchdog appointment earns its turn. Total 93.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's claim about the whole committee deliberately outruns evidence from one member; performance must keep that inference sincere.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D71-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"edcdf957115e00218d0aaf0e28868de148d8380c373863df4ffcc0b2da81253f\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 claims campaign consensus and A3 visual confidence; personal leadership appears only in A5's trailing 'under me.'\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 evades B2's slogan objection through its surroundings; A5 adjusts the endorsement language after B4 forbids endorsement.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 protect B from misrepresentation, but B has no further campaign responsibility or alternative offer.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 adds 'under me' after B4's refusal, but mainly repackages the original claim.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1's blue campaign poster and B2's disliked slogan provide concrete, playable points of disagreement.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's confidence in the slogan's 'surroundings' economically exploits B2's narrow objection.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"You like the blue campaign poster? I'll tell the volunteers they're happy with me in charge.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"The colour, yes. Could we work on the slogan together?\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Of course. I'm glad everyone's accepted my collaborative leadership.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"Let's each write one and let the volunteers choose.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Perfect. My first act as campaign leader is to delegate the decision.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D71 turns agreement with one campaign detail into confirmation of A's authority over the group.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary credit stealing would claim authorship of the poster or slogan without inferring acceptance of leadership.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 interprets B2's collaborative offer as acceptance of leadership, and A5 treats B4's shared vote as A's delegation.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This version absorbs helpful collaboration and shared voting into A's authority; alternative a absorbs explicit budget opposition.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised because original A1/A3 pursued proposal endorsement more clearly than leadership. Separate internal re-read: fidelity 5, A1/A3/A5 repeatedly infer authority; logic 5, collaboration becomes leadership and voting becomes delegation; relationship 4, B2/B4 seek shared authorship and choice; heightening 4, A5 claims executive power over the vote; specificity 5, A1/B2 establish a campaign poster task; brevity/wit 4, A5's delegation follows B4 exactly. Total 92.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The volunteers remain offstage, so A's assumption of their agreement must read as A's expansive inference.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D72-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"788ccd72c5746b8655c028d641b6c2a4ea2e29e6f34d102f4c9ca156eb8aa8f8\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 claims kitchen access through the team's tea; A5 uses a roof leak to claim another key.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 links B2's accounts role to tea, but A5 introduces the roof leak after B4's generic 'Where does that end?'\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 challenges A's remit, while B4 supplies a rhetorical question rather than a consequential operational response.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 expands from kitchen to roof, though B4 has not supplied the new problem driving that expansion.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1's kitchen key and A3's accounts staff needing alertness make the workplace demand easy to play.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's short chain from accounts to alertness to tea gives A's overreach an economical rationale.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 70\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Accounts keeps missing tea because the kitchen's locked. I need control of the keys.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"Here's a key. But the cleaners keep locking the kettle in their cupboard.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Then I'll approve their cleaning checklist. The kettle stays out.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"Their contractor only takes instructions from the building manager.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Then the building manager's instructions need my approval. Otherwise accounts can't reliably have tea.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D72 uses each newly encountered obstacle to justify control over an additional area.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Seeking enough access to solve a problem would stop once B supplies a working key.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised B2 grants access but reveals the locked-away kettle; A3 claims the cleaning checklist, and A5 claims approval over the building manager's instructions after B4 identifies the contractor's reporting restriction.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a turns a helpful access solution into growing administrative control; alternative b exploits a transport bottleneck to claim control of public roads.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected the rejected candidate's unsupported keyholder concern while preserving the office setting and original firstPass. Separate internal re-read under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, A3/A5 claim authority over successive areas; logic 5, B2's inaccessible kettle causes checklist control and B4's reporting restriction causes managerial oversight; relationship 4, B2 helps with access while B4 explains a limit on implementing A's demand; heightening 4, A5 places the building manager's instructions under accounts; specificity 5, A1's locked kitchen and B2's kettle give immediate practical stakes; brevity/wit 4, A5's tea justification sustains the disproportionate claim. Weighted total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns and causal hard gates pass; this is an internal re-read, not live testing.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A3 deliberately demands the whole checklist to solve one storage problem. A5's broader approval claim must remain sincerely grounded in reliable tea access.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D72-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"b8fc07756f38d5f679ade2e97c5bf6e29777f06b3f93d38fd1072519ef9a4f78\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 uses delivery delays to claim transport oversight, and A5 extends the claim to roads serving the rooms.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's meeting-room remit; A5 accepts the road expansion explicitly offered by B4.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 challenges scope, but B4's 'roads next?' chiefly supplies A with the final escalation.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 claims roads, but B4 supplies no new road problem requiring A to invent that claim.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes delivery delays and B2 identifies A as the meeting-room booker.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5's 'Only the ones leading to my rooms' makes an enormous claim sound carefully limited.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 74\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Your deliveries keep arriving during meetings. I need to approve the van schedule.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"You book the rooms. I'll send you our arrival times.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Send the routes too. I'll approve those so the times hold.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"The council keeps closing the access road. We can't schedule around that.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Give them my number. Road closures will need to fit my room bookings.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D72 treats operational difficulties as warrants for extending A's decision-making jurisdiction.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Overenthusiastic coordination could request information while leaving other people's authority intact.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 insists on approving routes, and A5 makes council closures answerable to room bookings rather than merely requesting updates.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b expands outward from a supplier to the council; alternative a grows inward through keys, access lists and facilities approval.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Replaced B4's suggested punchline with a concrete road-closure obstacle. Separate internal re-read: fidelity 5, A3/A5 claim successive approval powers; logic 5, arrival times prompt route control and closures prompt council control; relationship 4, B2 offers information and B4 identifies a real limit; heightening 5, A5 subordinates council decisions; specificity 4, meetings, vans and access roads establish the work; brevity/wit 4, A5 keeps the absurd reach tied to bookings. Total 93.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The council claim is a substantial comic leap, but it follows the newly supplied obstacle and A's established entitlement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D73-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"19de6ac06cedd49c718368ef7233027b5baeef0dcb6a99c45ef91ccf594335f2\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 rejects interchangeable hired help through the shared pencil mark; A5 values being together despite poor painting.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's hiring solution with shared history, and A5 directly accommodates B4's lack of painting skill.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 offers a practical substitute and B4 admits inadequacy, making A explain why this particular person matters.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 accepts a worse practical result after B4's admission, increasing commitment to companionship over competent help.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes painting an old kitchen, and A3's preserved pencil mark gives the shared history a playable object.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5's being 'terrible beside the mark together' turns B4's objection into a tender, economical invitation.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Come paint the old kitchen with me. Since Dad died, I barely see you.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I'll pay someone. I can't face painting over his height marks.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"We'll keep them. Come help me tape around them.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I'll probably just sit on the floor and cry.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Then I'll sit with you. We can leave the paint unopened; I don't want us only meeting to empty his house.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D73 makes this person's participation evidence that a shared connection continues to matter.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Recruiting useful help would favor the competent painter that B2 proposes.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 identifies lost contact after Dad's death; A3 protects the marks that trouble B2, and A5 relinquishes painting to secure time together after B4 anticipates grief.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a abandons a practical task to preserve a grieving family connection; alternative b changes an opening's arrangements so a father can witness A's achievement without meeting his former spouse.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected original's thin partner position and repeated final invitation. Added bereavement and clearing Dad's house as illustrative context. Separate internal re-read under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, A1/A5 make continued contact the purpose of help; logic 5, B2's threatened marks cause A3's protective task and B4's anticipated crying causes A5 to give up painting; relationship 4, B2 offers money while B4 reveals the emotional barrier; heightening 4, A5 sacrifices the task to ask for a connection beyond estate chores; specificity 5, A1's kitchen and B2's height marks locate the encounter; brevity/wit 4, A5's unopened paint concretely expresses the changed priority without a separate joke. Weighted total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns and causal hard gates pass on internal re-read.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The bereavement makes this a tender example with little overt comedy. A5 proposes time together without assuming that B is ready to enter the house.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D73-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"fb51f7f139d18011b0ea9585e07b437a2c6f37a8c8fc7f3472c1d58c1bc49ae2\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 makes knowing A the qualification for attending, and A5 asks for personal presence even with an unsuitable comment.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's lack of sculpture knowledge; A5 directly repurposes B4's fear of saying the wrong thing.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 express credible anxiety about attending an art opening, requiring A to lower the social demands.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 moves beyond waiving expertise to welcoming the feared mistake, provided B actually comes.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1's opening and ten-minute request combine with B2's sculpture reference to establish a playable invitation.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5's 'Say it in person' economically converts B4's exact objection into the desired commitment.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Dad, will you come to my opening? Even for ten minutes?\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I don't know anything about sculpture.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"You survived every school play. I still want you in my corner.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"Your mother will be there. We haven't spoken since the divorce.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Come before we open. I'll unpack the biggest piece with you. I want a memory of tonight with you in it too.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D73 seeks attendance from a personally significant person as an expression of the connection.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Seeking praise for artistic work would require a favorable response rather than merely showing up.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 makes the father's history of attending school plays the relevant qualification; A5 arranges a separate shared moment after B4 reveals why ordinary attendance is difficult.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b recruits a father's continuing support by creating a private part of a public achievement; alternative a lets a shared household task fall away so grieving relatives can spend time together.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected original's repeated reassurance by giving B a distinct family obstacle and A a consequential accommodation. Added a father and divorced parents as illustrative context. Separate internal re-read under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, A3/A5 seek evidence of continuing parental involvement; logic 5, B2's expertise concern prompts A3's attendance history and B4's divorce prompts A5's earlier visit; relationship 4, B4 supplies a credible reluctance that A accommodates; heightening 4, A5 turns ten-minute attendance into a specific shared contribution and remembered moment; specificity 5, A1's opening and A5's unpacking offer an immediate activity; brevity/wit 4, A3's school-play comparison earns its gentle humor from B2. Weighted total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns and causal hard gates pass on internal re-read.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"An early visit may not remove every difficulty created by the divorce. A5 offers a workable opening for B's response rather than claiming the family tension is resolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D74-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"63e668cb29f2aa87eb9981164b12237328dc39dc5b6a59e29158a67367e4fd54\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 delays B's departure for relational repair, but A5 shifts to what will let A put a coat on.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 distinguishes people from B2's spotless kitchen; A5 answers B4's task question with a requested sentence.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 evade emotional discussion through completed cleaning and remaining chores.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 specifies a sentence but does not sharpen the cost of keeping B present, and changes whose departure is foregrounded.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes finished dishes and imminent departure; B2 keeps the kitchen situation concrete.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's 'people who cleaned it' efficiently redirects B2's practical answer toward the relationship.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"The dishes are done. Before you go, are we actually okay?\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I stayed to clean up. I've done that.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"I know. Could you stay for the part that isn't the kitchen?\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I've got ten minutes before the last bus.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Then sit with me for nine. I don't want the last thing we fixed tonight to be the sink.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D74 keeps the other person present for relational repair after their practical reason to stay has ended.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Seeking reassurance alone could be satisfied later without trying to extend the current encounter.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 explicitly requests continued presence after B2 declares the cleaning complete, and A5 bargains for nine of B4's ten remaining minutes.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a bargains within B's departure deadline; alternative b openly accepts lateness to avoid leaving an unresolved argument.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the ambiguous coat exit and supplied a bounded departure cost. Separate internal re-read: fidelity 5, A3 requests staying beyond cleanup; logic 5, B2's completed task and B4's bus deadline determine A's requests; relationship 4, B has fulfilled a commitment and needs transport; heightening 4, A5 claims nine remaining minutes; specificity 5, dishes and the last bus are concrete; brevity/wit 4, A5's sink comparison grows from the cleaning. Total 92.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 requests time for repair without guaranteeing reconciliation; its sink phrasing needs an unpolished, sincere delivery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D74-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"7a3ec7282e755f257fe6bebc81604ea6f976e420729447bbfb17429bee855eaf\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 holds off the taxi over last night's conflict, and A5 chooses lateness over departing unrepaired.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 contests B2's claim that the conversation is finished; A5 explicitly accepts B4's threatened lateness.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 considers the discussion complete and B4 wants to protect their shared arrival time.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B4 makes delay costly, and A5 accepts that cost rather than releasing the repair demand.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes an imminent taxi and an unresolved previous-night exchange, though the destination remains open.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's distinction between stopping and finishing supplies a concise relational turn without extra explanation.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Don't call the taxi yet. We're packed, but we haven't settled last night.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I apologised for telling my sister about your debt.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Stay a minute. I can't face her checking what I order tonight.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"She's already paid for dinner. We'll be late.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Then we'll be late. Sit with me and help me believe you're coming as my partner, not to report how grateful I am.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D74 subordinates departure to feeling that the relationship has been repaired.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Winning an argument would seek agreement on the disputed facts or position rather than preventing an unrepaired goodbye.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 asks B to stay after B2 treats an apology as sufficient; A5 delays departure to address trust in B when B4's paid dinner makes the disclosure's consequences immediate.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b accepts shared lateness to seek renewed trust before facing B's sister; alternative a negotiates within the last-bus deadline after a completed household task.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected original's generic dispute and repeated refusal to leave. Added a disclosed debt and dinner with B's sister as illustrative context, retaining the taxi, unfinished previous-night argument and postponed departure used by the supplied pairs. Separate internal re-read under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, A1/A3/A5 keep B present beyond completed packing for relational repair; logic 5, B2's disclosure prompts A3's fear of scrutiny and B4's paid dinner prompts A5's concern about being monitored for gratitude; relationship 4, B2 offers an apology and B4 protects a paid engagement while A identifies damaged trust; heightening 4, A5 accepts lateness and asks for a changed understanding of B's role; specificity 5, A1's taxi and B2/B4's sister, debt and dinner make the conflict concrete; brevity/wit 4, A5's reporting concern develops the original breach without a detachable joke. Weighted total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns and causal hard gates pass on internal re-read.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 fears further reporting without evidence that B intends it. The line should expose damaged trust and invite repair, leaving B room to disagree.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D75-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"21bd0354540522aab22d3a231ff18615f50986bff19baabf9b6e39898b17e6a2\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 asks what the empty seat felt like; A3 rejects the transport explanation as a substitute for emotional acknowledgment.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's cancelled train directly, and A5 recognizes B4's 'let down' before asking B to remain with that understanding.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 offers a credible reason for absence and B4 then identifies the hurt, changing A from eliciting acknowledgment to sustaining it.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 extends B4's acknowledgment rather than deepening its specificity or consequence substantially.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1's missed concert and empty seat give the emotional cost a concrete, immediately playable image.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's timetable-versus-seat distinction compresses the disagreement into objects already supplied by A1 and B2.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 89\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"You apologised for missing my concert. What do you think the empty seat felt like?\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"My train was cancelled.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"I know the timetable. I'm asking about the seat.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"You felt let down. I watched the recording this morning.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Then you saw me look at the door before the encore. Can you understand how it felt to keep expecting you?\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D75 requires acknowledgment of the felt impact of an event beyond an apology or causal explanation.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Demanding an admission of fault would contest whether the cancelled train excuses B's absence.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 does not dispute B2's train cancellation; revised A5 uses B4's watched recording to ask for recognition of repeated disappointed expectation rather than responsibility for the transport failure.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a turns B's effort to watch the performance into a way to recognize the hurt visible during it; alternative b develops apparent indifference into the private emotional work of rewriting unanswered messages.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected original's static A5 and presumption of renewed defensiveness. Kept the concert setting and gave B4 a concrete act of involvement that A5 can use. Separate internal re-read under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, A3/A5 seek felt impact beyond the transport explanation; logic 5, B2 prompts the timetable distinction and B4's recording prompts a specific visible moment; relationship 4, B4 both acknowledges hurt and demonstrates continued interest; heightening 4, A5 develops general disappointment into repeatedly expecting arrival during the performance; specificity 5, A1's seat and A5's door before the encore make the cost playable; brevity/wit 4, A3's existing contrast stays economical and A5 adds concrete evidence without an unrelated joke. Weighted total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns and causal hard gates pass on internal re-read.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 assumes the recording shows A looking toward the door. That is a new illustrative fact, and the request should allow B to distinguish noticing the glance from understanding its meaning.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D75-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"aaa74bd875eef0ebf9ed0079d44b0729dd68b0732bab3ba75f12fd8ddcc8cd00\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 seeks understanding of six weeks' hurt, and A3 explains the emotional uncertainty hidden by B2's broken-phone account.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's repair explanation, and A5 explicitly recognizes B4 naming the fear that B stopped caring.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 initially explains the silence and B4 then articulates A's interpretation, creating a credible movement toward understanding.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 2,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5's 'That's the bit I needed' closes the request after B4 rather than developing the established pursuit.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies six weeks of silence and B2 a broken phone, though the relationship's circumstances remain unspecified.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's missing 'repair notice' turns B2's technical explanation into a concise account of emotional uncertainty.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 84\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Tell me you understand why six weeks of silence hurt.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"My phone broke.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"For me, the silence didn't come with a repair notice.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"You thought I'd stopped caring.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Yes. Can you understand why I kept rewriting a message you'd never answered, trying to become easier to reply to?\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D75 presses for recognition of the emotional cost concealed behind an event's practical explanation.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Requesting better future communication would seek a contact plan rather than acknowledgment of past hurt.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 translates B2's broken phone into unexplained silence, and revised A5 develops B4's named fear into repeated self-editing.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b exposes private self-doubt created by silence; alternative a focuses on B remaining with the disappointment represented by an empty concert seat.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised A5 to let B4's accurate understanding open a more specific cost. Separate internal re-read: fidelity 5, A5 requests acknowledgment of self-erasure; logic 5, B4's stopped-caring interpretation prompts its lived consequence; relationship 4, B moves from explanation to empathy; heightening 4, A5 discloses repeated rewriting; specificity 5, the unanswered message supplies a concrete behavior; brevity/wit 4, A3's repair notice remains earned. Total 92.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The revised ending is emotionally exposed and longer than the other turns; it should sound discovered rather than delivered as a polished speech.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D76-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"f677f3addaf43851b293f9b831ea0cc2474a03c0f4ca9c50f96799b75fab0be0\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 has already accepted the job, and A3 explicitly distinguishes the settled job from B's missing gladness.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's permission distinction; A5 answers B4's happiness question and asks B to share that hope.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 respects A's autonomy and B4 asks about A's welfare, giving B a credible caring position.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 admits uncertainty with 'I think so' and still asks B4's concerned questioner for active hope.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes an accepted new job, while A3 makes the immediate missing response clear.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's 'I'm missing your gladness' succinctly separates emotional approval from B2's unnecessary permission.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Dad, I've accepted the museum job. Can you be pleased I'm leaving the workshop?\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"You're thirty. You don't need me to release you.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"I need the person who taught me to make things to think this counts.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I'll pack your tools. You might want to come back.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Could you sharpen them for the museum instead? Send me off expecting me to do well.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D76 pursues emotionally meaningful approval after the decision no longer requires permission.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Seeking authorization would leave acceptance of the job conditional on B's consent.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 has already accepted the job; A3 identifies the father's role as A's teacher, and A5 asks him to redirect B4's practical help from preparing for retreat toward expressing confidence.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a asks a father and teacher to express confidence through preparing tools for a career departure; alternative b asks a mother to extend partial approval to A's partner and shared home through a toast.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected original's unspecified emotional authority and repeated encouragement request. Added a father, family workshop and museum job as illustrative context. Separate internal re-read under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, A1's accepted job and A3's teacher appeal distinguish blessing from permission; logic 5, B2's release framing causes A3's explanation and B4's packed tools prompt A5's request to prepare them for success; relationship 4, B4 offers useful help while anticipating A's return; heightening 4, A5 asks B to embody confidence in the actual departure; specificity 5, A1's workshop and A5's sharpening make the approval request playable; brevity/wit 4, A5 repurposes the tools without adding a separate joke. Weighted total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns and causal hard gates pass on internal re-read.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The museum role is left broad, though the tools imply practical making or conservation work. B4's fallback offer can carry care as well as doubt; A's interpretation need not be the only valid one.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D76-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"92c47f0ac6459c4e274a6537fbd7796db763f84c856fc773d60da3bb9430c943\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1/A3 seek a blessing after signing the lease, but A5's bottle direction makes the emotional approval less legible.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 responds to B2's settled decision, and A5 uses B4's support, though redirecting it toward the bottle lacks a clear need.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 notes autonomy and B4 offers support, but no concrete concern explains the emotional distance.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 2,\n            \"evidence\": \"After B4 supplies support, A5 changes its delivery direction without increasing the relational consequence.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1's signed lease and unopened wine establish a concrete attempted celebration.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5's 'toward the bottle' is short, but its joke does not clearly develop the distinction established in A3.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 69\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Mum, we signed the lease. Say it's good news before I open the wine.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"You don't need my permission to move in with Sam.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"I know. I still want my mother to be pleased.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I'm pleased for you. I'm still getting used to Sam.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Could you toast both of us? I want to remember you welcoming our home, not just tolerating my address.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D76 seeks a valued person's emotional endorsement of a decision already made independently.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Demanding a ceremonial phrase would make the toast itself sufficient regardless of what B approves.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised B4 separates pleasure for A from acceptance of Sam, and A5 asks the toast to acknowledge their shared home.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b gives a mother a specific reservation about a partner; alternative a uses a confidant's concern about A's happiness at work.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Clarified the illustrative relationship as mother and adult child, and made B's partial support consequential. Separate internal re-read: fidelity 5, A1/A3 seek approval after signing; logic 5, B2 prompts the emotional distinction and B4 prompts a toast to both partners; relationship 5, B supports A while hesitating over Sam; heightening 4, A5 asks acceptance of the shared home; specificity 5, lease, mother, partner and wine are concrete; brevity/wit 4, A5's address distinction follows the conflict. Total 95.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 asks for more approval than B currently offers; the scene should preserve B's freedom to respond rather than imply the toast settles everything.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D77-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"b882274d2fabcad132fa3f13bb5ec46affcb2a2b6a2b6746c202bb2548be1201\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 requests shared forms and A5 places their names together, but A3 argues from tiredness more than the relationship.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's expertise excuse, and A5 answers B4's 'Which half?' with a page, though its actual burden is unclear.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 relies on A's competence and B4 agrees to participate, giving A a helpful opening.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 makes the names emotionally significant but does not establish what additional responsibility B is taking.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 identifies care-home forms, while A5's unnamed page leaves the practical division vague.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's 'I understand enough to be tired' economically challenges B2's use of expertise as an exemption.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Take half Mum's care-home forms. Being her children can't mean I'm the paperwork child.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"You understand them better.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Then sit beside me. I'll explain, and you can write.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"All right. It asks for one main contact.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Put yourself. I'll help with every answer, but Mum needs to know she can reach either of us.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D77 recruits shared responsibility by making participation an obligation and expression of the relationship.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple task delegation could assign forms on efficiency grounds without invoking shared family responsibility.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 grounds the request in both being Mum's children, and A5 makes B the contact while promising A's continued help.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a negotiates concrete ongoing sibling responsibility; alternative b uses a jointly accumulated box of letters to ask for shared physical and emotional carrying.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Clarified the illustrative relationship as siblings helping Mum and made B4 disclose a consequential form choice. Separate internal re-read: fidelity 5, A1/A5 frame work through shared family ties; logic 5, B2 prompts supported writing and B4 prompts contact responsibility; relationship 5, B agrees while A continues helping; heightening 4, A5 expands from writing to being reachable; specificity 5, the main-contact field is playable; brevity/wit 4, A1's paperwork child captures the imbalance. Total 95.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"Naming B as main contact remains A's proposal; the dialogue does not establish B's availability or complete that decision.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D77-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"38ecf764d58da4b501948e840d0fc6282cca75757b820fe1cd1daa162dcf153b\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 answers individual packing responsibility with ten years of shared accumulation, making A5's carrying request relational.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 directly counters B2's 'You packed it'; A5 answers B4 with carrying, though 'while we decide' introduces an unspecified decision.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 disclaims responsibility and B4 asks what is wanted, allowing A to translate shared history into participation.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 extends carrying into shared deciding, but leaves the decision and added consequence implicit.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 gives actors a box with two handles, and A3 supplies ten years of relationship history.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's 'We filled it' crisply overturns B2's 'You packed it,' earning A5's literal and relational 'your side.'\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 85\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Help me sort our letters before the removal van comes.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"You kept them. I thought you'd want the box.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"We filled it over ten years. I shouldn't have to decide alone what survives us.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I'll take the box. I just can't read them today.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Then choose a day we'll open it together. Taking it away can't mean you inherit all the deciding.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D77 makes a shared history the reason B should carry part of a present burden.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D73 would chiefly seek B's presence as proof of connection; this scene asks B to assume a share of what their connection produced.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 frames decisions about their jointly accumulated letters as shared responsibility; A5 refuses to let B4's offer of sole custody transfer all the emotional work to B.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b turns an offer to take the whole physical burden into a request for shared future decisions after a separation; alternative a distributes present paperwork and continuing family responsibility between siblings.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected original's undefined decision and repeated carrying instruction. Added removal-day sorting after a separation as illustrative context. Separate internal re-read under coach-1.0: fidelity 5, A3/A5 explicitly make deciding about shared letters a joint burden; logic 5, B2's presumed ownership prompts A3's shared-history claim and B4's custody offer plus reading limit prompts A5's later joint appointment; relationship 4, B4 offers help while setting an emotional boundary that A respects; heightening 4, A5 commits to sharing future emotional work even when offered immediate relief; specificity 5, A1's removal van and B4's unread box make the decision and deadline concrete; brevity/wit 4, A5's inherited deciding develops B4's offered custody economically. Weighted total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns and causal hard gates pass on internal re-read.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A3's 'what survives us' is somewhat literary. A5 leaves both the date and the decision about the letters open, so B's offer is not treated as consent to a future meeting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D78-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"0043d387fd965e331460d0e05110b6e427265b7201348459f5c1471c4a7ae34e\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 2,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 asks what to write after B's name and A3 explicitly invokes a 'relationship' box, effectively requesting a label.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's name-only response with the form field, and A5 turns B4's blank into a seating question.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 consistently avoid definition, but provide little beyond refusal for A to interpret.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 gives B4's blank a seating consequence, though the practical connection between invitation and seating is thin.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 2,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's relationship box on a wedding invitation feels constructed to force the card question rather than a natural logistical need.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5's sitting 'beside the blank' is compact but prioritizes wordplay over the concrete seating decision.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 56\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"For your sister's wedding, should I book us one room or two?\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"One is cheaper.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Double bed, then, or shall I ask for twins?\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"Twins. I don't want to confuse things.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Right. At breakfast, do I sit with your family or wait for you in the lobby?\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D78 probes how B defines their connection through practical choices without directly requesting a relationship label.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Asking for a label through a form remains a direct definition request despite the administrative dis